<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    Surfaces: everything the app draws content *on*. Cards, the floating cards of the overlays and
    the toasts, the scrim that dims the editor under a modal, the framed lists, the hairline rows,
    and the status chip.

    Two design laws are enforced here rather than at the call sites:
      - Flat elevation is a 1px SurfaceLine border and no shadow at all. Only a surface that
        genuinely floats over another one casts a shadow, and then it casts the popover shadow or
        the modal shadow, never an invented one.
      - The motion budget is a budget. Every `Transitions` below binds a bare alias
        (`ModalTransitions`, ...) with `DynamicResource`; MotionResourceBinder points that alias at
        the full or the reduced set once at startup, so a view never chooses between them.

    Each animated surface is driven by an `.open` / `.shown` class rather than by `IsVisible`
    alone: `IsVisible` false collapses a control instantly and gives a transition nothing to run
    on, whereas a class flipping re-evaluates the Opacity setter and the transition plays.
  -->

  <!-- The window itself. `:is(Window)` rather than `Window`, because an Avalonia type selector
       matches the exact type and every window in the app is a subclass. -->
  <Style Selector=":is(Window)">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceCanvasBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Cards ============================================================================= -->

  <!-- The panel every group of content sits on. Radius 8, 1px line, 14 padding, no shadow. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.card">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfacePanelBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPanel}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingCard}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A dashboard card. Cards are keyed by drive identity and never reordered by a scan; a newly
       found device animates in at the end of the list, height only, 160 ms ease-out. The
       transition lives here so the dashboard has exactly one place that decides it. Scanning is
       manual, but a scan still inserts cards, so the insert is still earned.

       Three things it adds to `card`:

       - A FIXED HEIGHT. Every state renders at `HeightDeviceCard`, so a scan that flips a card to
         Scanning - or back - cannot move the button the pointer is already on (mockup 2e).
       - PADDING 0. The 2px status rail is flush against the card's left edge, which means inside
         the border but *outside* the padding, so the padding moves inward onto the content and off
         the Border that draws the frame.
       - ClipToBounds. The rail and the scan bar run to the card's own edges; the 8px radius has to
         cut their corners rather than let them square off the card.

       The height is also what the insert animation runs on. A transition never fires on an initial
       value, so a card that is simply born at its natural height appears rather than animates: the
       view authors `collapsed` in markup and drops it after attach, and the class - never
       `IsVisible` - is what re-evaluates the setter and lets the 160 ms play.

       TOP-ALIGNED, and that is the insert animation's business rather than a layout preference.
       A Layoutable with an explicit Height and the default Stretch is *centred* in its slot, and a
       card inserted into a UniformGrid row that already holds a full-height sibling is arranged in
       a 212-tall slot from the first frame: at 0 height it would sit 106 down the row and grow
       outward from its middle, which is not "animates in at the end of the list". -->
  <Style Selector="Border.deviceCard">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource HeightDeviceCard}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
    <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource ListInsertTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.deviceCard.collapsed">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The status rail: 2px, flush on the card's left edge, painted with the status *fill* role
       rather than its text role - it is a band of colour, not a label on a tint.

       It is card composition rather than a control shape, so it is a style here and not a
       ControlTheme: nothing clicks it, it has no states of its own, and the only thing it varies
       is which of two roles fills it. Scanning has no rail at all - a scan in flight is transient,
       and "no red, no spinner" (mockup 2e) is what the absence of a colour says.

       THE ABSENCE IS A TRANSPARENT FILL, NOT A HIDDEN CONTROL. "Status changes cross-fade the
       status pill and the card status fill over 220ms; text swaps are instant" (mockup 2b) is a
       claim about the rail appearing and disappearing as much as about it changing colour, and
       `IsVisible` collapses a control instantly - it gives the transition nothing to run on, the
       same trap the overlay surfaces above avoid with an `.open` class. So the rail is always
       present and always laid out, and every state change is a Background change the 220ms
       BrushTransition below can fade. The caption beside it still swaps instantly, as the design
       asks.

       The bare alias, never StatusFillTransitionsFull: MotionResourceBinder points it at the full
       or the reduced set once at startup. Both sets keep the cross-fade - reduce-motion drops
       movement, not fades. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.cardStatusRail">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource WidthCardStatusRail}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource StatusFillTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.cardStatusRail.ok">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusOkBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.cardStatusRail.error">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusErrorBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The per-state sentence under a card's status line. Quiet by default, so a card that has
       something to say is not automatically the loudest thing on the dashboard.

       `.error` is the one that is drawn as a box, because there the sentence is the remedy: an
       error tint inside a stronger error border, the same two-strength reading of a status hue the
       status pill uses. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.cardExplanation">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,9" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.cardExplanation.error">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusErrorTintBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusErrorTintBorderBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The well a card's device silhouette is drawn in (docs/design/handoff.md:117, "92x56 device
       art, abstract key-grid drawing, not a photo"). Control radius, a 1px line and 6 of padding,
       exactly as the mock draws it; the drawing inside is filled with SurfaceThumbnail, which is
       a step away from this face in both variants so the board reads against its well.

       The face is SurfaceBar. The mock puts the well one step *up* from the card on dark and one
       step *down* on light, and no single role does both; SurfaceBar is the one that lands on the
       mock's dark value exactly and within a shade of its light one, while keeping the drawing
       legible against it either way. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.deviceArtFrame">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBarBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The app mark's tile in the app bar: an 18px accent square at keycap radius carrying the
       700 mono "K" (`.logoMark`). It is the only accent fill in the chrome, which is the point —
       the accent means "the app itself" here, one of its two sanctioned meanings. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.logoTile">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="18" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="18" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusKeycap}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A card that floats: the toasts, the loading indicator, the feature panels. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.overlayCard">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfacePanelBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPanel}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingCard}" />
    <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowPopover}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The same card when it is genuinely modal — a spec 11 panel over the scrim. It sits higher,
       so it casts the deeper shadow; everything else it inherits from `overlayCard`. It carries no
       width: every spec 11 panel sizes itself to its own content (480 for Search Keys, 520 for Tap
       and Hold, ...), and a width here would silently override all of them. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.overlayCard.modalCard">
    <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowModal}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The message box's own two widths, and the only card in the app that has more than one.
       330 is the ordinary box — a title, a sentence and a row of answers. `.wide` is 420, which
       docs/design/handoff.md gives the leave-with-unsaved modal: its body is a paragraph carrying
       mono counts, and its three answers are named by outcome rather than Yes/No/Cancel, so the
       narrow card would wrap both. The view binds the class off the request (`IsWide`), so which
       one a box takes is the caller's statement rather than the view's guess.

       A literal rather than a token: these are the widths of one surface, not a measure anything
       else in the app lines up with, and Themes/Geometry.axaml is for the latter. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.messageBoxCard">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="330" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.messageBoxCard.wide">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="420" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Modal and scrim ===================================================================
       The feature panels of specs/11-feature-dialogs.md render inline over the editor rather than
       as separate windows, so the scrim is what makes them read as modal: it dims the editor and,
       being hit-test visible, swallows every click aimed at it. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.overlayScrim">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ScrimBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource ScrimTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.overlayScrim.open">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The host the open panel is rendered into. It fades separately from the scrim so the panel
       arrives on an already-dimmed editor rather than with it. -->
  <Style Selector="ContentControl.overlayHost">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource ModalTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ContentControl.overlayHost.open">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Popovers ==========================================================================
       120 ms fade plus a 2 px rise. The rise is the RenderTransform half of `PopoverTransitions`,
       which the reduced set drops; the `.open` setter still lands on `none`, so with reduce-motion
       the surface simply appears in place. -->
  <Style Selector="ContentControl.popover">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="RenderTransform" Value="translateY(2px)" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource PopoverTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ContentControl.popover.open">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="RenderTransform" Value="none" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The flyout surfaces Avalonia builds for us (the Special Actions menu, combo box drop-downs).
       They are popovers, so the budget governs them from here; Avalonia raises no open/closed
       pseudo-class on a presenter, so today this only bounds any property change the presenter
       makes of its own accord. -->
  <Style Selector="FlyoutPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource PopoverTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="MenuFlyoutPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource PopoverTransitions}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Toast =============================================================================
       In and out at 180 ms, with a 5 s dwell between them that NotificationOverlay owns — the
       toast has always carried its own timeout (specs/11-feature-dialogs.md §11.9), so there is
       one timer, not two. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.toast">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="RenderTransform" Value="translateY(6px)" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource ToastTransitions}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.toast.shown">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="RenderTransform" Value="none" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Status chip =======================================================================
       A pill whose fill states what the app found: connected, an advisory, a failure, or demo
       mode. It stays a Border — a readout is not a button, and the 220 ms cross-fade the design
       asks for is a Transitions set naming Border.Background / Border.BorderBrush, which are
       different AvaloniaProperty instances from a TemplatedControl's identically named ones.

       A ControlTheme may target any StyledElement, so `StatusPill` carries the geometry, the
       cross-fade and all four severity faces; this bridge declares nothing else, because a Style
       setter outranks a ControlTheme setter and would take the variants straight back off it.
       The caption inside is still the view's own TextBlock on `statusOk`/`statusWarning`/... — a
       Border has no Foreground to inherit down. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.statusChip">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource StatusPill}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Keyboard legend ====================================================================
       A key the user is meant to press, printed inline: the `⌥1`-`⌥5` hints on the editor's layer
       segments today, and the shortcut table of mockup 2b when it lands. Mono, because a legend is
       a literal rather than the app talking.

       It sits here beside the status chip rather than in Buttons.axaml because it is the other
       non-button lozenge of Themes/ControlThemes/Pills.axaml: a ContentControl and not a Button,
       for the same reason the chip is a Border — it is a readout, never a target. -->
  <Style Selector="ContentControl.kbd">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource KbdChip}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Framed lists and hairline rows ==================================================== -->

  <!-- A scrollable area that has to read as one field: the recorded macro steps, the token
       picker's results. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.listFrame">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4" />
  </Style>

  <!-- One row of a settings panel or of the pedal table. A separator belongs *between* rows, so it
       is a top border on every row but the first: a bottom border would leave a trailing hairline
       under the last row, and a top border on all of them draws one flush inside the card's own
       top edge, above the first row, with nothing above it to separate.

       Every listRow in the app is the root of an ItemsControl's ItemTemplate, so the row sits in a
       generated ContentPresenter and it is that presenter, not the Border, that is a sibling of
       the other rows in the items panel — which is what :nth-child can count. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.listRow">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,1,0,0" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0,12" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ContentPresenter:nth-child(1) > Border.listRow">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Pedal entry ======================================================================= -->

  <!-- The panel of specs/12-savant-elite.md §5 that opens under the input being programmed. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.entryPanel">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBarBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPanel}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingCard}" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,8,0,4" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The entry box itself: a read-only, selectable display of what has been recorded so far.
       Deliberately not a TextBox — focus in one auto-suspends the capture service, so typing into
       the pedal would record nothing — which is the same shape, and the same reason, as the Tap and
       Hold action fields. It is therefore a `TokenField`, and it is open only while it is armed, so
       the view carries the `armed` class outright rather than binding it.

       The one thing it does not take from the theme is its height: a pedal macro wraps over several
       lines, and 44 is the room that leaves. Padding is the theme's 13x8 rather than the 12x8 this
       box used to set for itself — one pixel, and one fewer place that decides a field's rhythm. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.entryBox">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource TokenField}" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="44" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Toast severity ======================================================================
       Mockup 1k draws exactly two toasts: a success and an advisory. They differ in one dot and
       one border, and in nothing else - the card, the shadow and the 180 ms fade above are shared,
       because an advisory is a different thing to say, not a louder way of saying it.

       Amber is the only warning colour in this app. There is deliberately no error variant: a
       failure is a message box, which the user has to answer, and never a notice that vanishes
       after five seconds. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.toast.advisory">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The dot. 8x8 on the 4px grid - the mock's 7px rounded to the grid, because a one-off
       dimension cannot be a named token from here (Themes/Geometry.axaml owns those) and 7 is off
       the grid the whole design is built on. `RadiusPill` is what makes it a circle. It is nudged
       down to sit on the title's optical centre rather than on the line box's top. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.toastDot">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="8" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="8" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,4,0,0" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPill}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusOkBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.toastDot.advisory">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Suppression row =====================================================================
       The "Don't ask this again" opt-out of mockup 1k, boxed so it reads as a preference rather
       than as one of the dialog's answers. An inset face, because it is a hole in the card and not
       a thing on top of it - the same rule the fields follow. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.suppressionRow">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Numbered step marker =================================================================
       The tile behind each digit of the empty state's connection steps (mockup 1d). 20x20 on the
       4px grid, at keycap radius, so a column of them reads as a rail down the left of the list
       and every step starts on the same x whether its sentence wraps or not.

       Raised rather than inset: a marker sits ON the panel, it is not a hole in it, and the raised
       face is the one that stays visible on both ramps against `panel` (#16191C dark, #FFFFFF
       light). It carries no accent — a step number is a marker, and the accent means selection or
       "you changed this", neither of which a step is. The digit's own type is `.stepNumber`. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.stepMarker">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusKeycap}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
  </Style>
</Styles>
